<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Willem Barentsz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1550–<a href="/years/1597">1597</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as William Barents, Barentz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Willem Barentsz was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> navigator, cartographer and Arctic explorer, born around 1550 on the island of Terschelling. He led three expeditions in search of a Northeast passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>: in 1594 and 1595 he reached Novaya Zemlya and the Kara Sea but was turned back by ice. On the third, in 1596, his crew discovered Bear Island and Spitsbergen, then became trapped by ice off Novaya Zemlya and wintered in a lodge built from driftwood and ship's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, Het Behouden Huys. In June 1597 the survivors set out in two open boats, and Barentsz died on 20 June. The Barents Sea is named after him, and the lodge was found in 1871.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/willem-barentsz.jpg" alt="Willem Barentsz" width="330" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified engraver · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Willem_Barents_(1883).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Barentsz was born around 1550 in the village of Formerum on Terschelling. Barentsz was a patronymic, short for Barentszoon, meaning Barent's son. A cartographer by trade, he sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> to complete an atlas of the Mediterranean region, which he published with Petrus Plancius.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 5 June 1594 Barentsz left Texel in the small ship Mercury, one of three ships sent in separate directions towards the Kara Sea. He reached the west coast of Novaya Zemlya and followed it northward until icebergs forced him back. In 1595 Prince Maurice of Orange named him chief pilot of a new expedition of seven ships, six of them carrying goods to trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It passed between the Siberian coast and Vaygach Island, met about twenty Samoyed people, and turned back when the Kara Sea proved frozen. Two sailors were killed by a polar bear on States Island, and the voyage was largely considered a failure.</p><p>In 1596 the States-General stopped subsidising such voyages and offered a reward for anyone who navigated the Northeast Passage. The Town Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> equipped two ships under Jan Rijp and Jacob van Heemskerk, with Barentsz in command. They found Bear Island on 9 June and Spitsbergen on 17 June, charting parts of its coast. After a disagreement on 1 July, Rijp sailed due north to cross the pole while Barentsz went northeast, reaching Novaya Zemlya on 17 July. His ship became stuck in ice, and the sixteen-man crew wintered in a lodge of driftwood and ship's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> measuring 7.8 by 5.5 metres, Het Behouden Huys. In January 1597 they recorded the polar mirage now called the Novaya Zemlya effect.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In early June 1597 the weakened survivors set out in two open boats. Barentsz died on 20 June at about 47, and it is not known whether he was buried on Novaya Zemlya or at sea. The survivors reached the Kola Peninsula, where Rijp's merchant vessel rescued them by chance, and only twelve men remained when they reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> on 1 November 1597.</p><p>In 1871 the Norwegian seal hunter Elling Carlsen found the lodge still standing, and in 1876 Charles L. W. Gardiner recovered 112 objects, including a message from Barentsz and Van Heemskerk, later given to the Rijksmuseum. The former Murmean Sea was renamed the Barents Sea in 1853. Gerrit de Veer, the ship's carpenter, and Jan Huyghen van Linschoten, who sailed on the first two voyages, both published journals.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Willem Barentsz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
